// Synchronisation through volatile flags, with the data in a normal location.
//
// Source: Ševčík, "Program Transformations in Weak Memory Models" (PhD thesis,
//         Edinburgh 2008), Example 2.6. Java volatiles u and v are written here as release stores and
//         acquire loads, which is all the example needs of them.
//
// Every pair of accesses to x is ordered by a release-acquire pair on u or v, so
// the program is data-race-free. If r1 = 1, thread 2 has run x := 2 after
// x := 1, so r2 = 2: r1 = 1, r2 = 1 is FORBIDDEN. The thesis' point is that a C
// compiler replacing r2 := x by the constant 1 breaks this.
//
x := 0; u := 0; v := 0;
{
  x := 1;
  u.store(1, rel);
  r1 := v.load(acq);
  if (r1 = 1) {
    r2 := x
  }
} ||| {
  r3 := u.load(acq);
  if (r3 = 1) {
    x := 2;
    v.store(1, rel)
  }
}
%%
forbid (r1 = 1 && r2 = 1) [Sevcik]
